# Night of a Thousand Pixels - local development environment.
#
# Copy this file to .env (`make setup` does it for you) and edit as needed.
# The Makefile does `-include .env` followed by a bare `export`, so every make
# recipe - and docker compose - inherits whatever is set here.
#
#   NEVER commit a real secret. .env is git-ignored; this file is not.
#   Production reads its configuration from the deployment environment and
#   never reads .env at all.
#
# Syntax note: make parses this file, so a value must not contain a literal
# `#` (make treats the rest of the line as a comment) and a literal `$` must
# be doubled as `$$`.

# --- Agent slot --------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately not set here. Several agents run `make dev` on one machine, so
# ports and container names are derived from AGENT_SLOT (default 0):
#
#   app port       8080 + SLOT*10      templ proxy   7331 + SLOT*10
#   postgres port  5433 + SLOT*10      compose proj  nap-a<SLOT>
#   oidc port      9000 + SLOT*10
#
# Run `AGENT_SLOT=1 make dev` rather than pinning ports in this file.

# --- Required by internal/config/config.go -----------------------------------
# Three of the seven keys the server requires belong to the slot, not to you:
#
#   DATABASE_URL      postgres://nap:nap@localhost:<POSTGRES_PORT>/nap?sslmode=disable
#   PORT              <APP_PORT>
#   ORIGIN            http://localhost:<TEMPL_PROXY_PORT>
#   OAUTH_ISSUER_URL  http://localhost:<OIDC_PORT>/oidc
#
# The Makefile assigns all four from AGENT_SLOT, so setting them HERE HAS NO
# EFFECT - make prints a warning and uses the derived value. They are three
# views of one machine-local stack: pin one and you get an app on this slot's
# port talking to another slot's database, which fails silently and is the
# whole reason the slot scheme exists.
#
# For a genuine one-off, pass it on the command line, which wins over
# everything:  make test-integration DATABASE_URL=postgres://...

# OIDC provider. There is no default and no provider hostname baked into the
# binary: local development and the test suite both point this at a mock OIDC
# server, so this is a localhost URL, not a real identity provider.
#
# OAUTH_ISSUER_URL is NOT set here - it belongs to the agent slot, like PORT
# and ORIGIN, and the Makefile derives it as http://localhost:<OIDC_PORT>/oidc.
# `make dev` starts the mock provider (docker-compose.dev.yml) on that port.
#
# The mock provider is not a bypass. It speaks real OIDC and the application
# runs its real authentication path against it - discovery, JWKS, PKCE S256,
# code exchange, RS256 verification, issuer, audience, expiry and nonce. The
# only thing that changes is which issuer is named. So a misconfigured issuer
# in production means sign-in is BROKEN, never BYPASSED.
#
# The client id and secret are shared between the app and the mock provider,
# which is why they are here rather than derived: the provider is handed the
# same two values and rejects any client that does not match.
OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=nap-local-dev
OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=mock-oidc-client-secret-not-a-real-secret

# Session cookie signing key. MUST be at least 32 characters or the server
# refuses to start (see minCookieSecretLen in internal/config/config.go).
# This placeholder is fake and is for local development only - generate a real
# one per deployed environment with:  openssl rand -base64 48
COOKIE_SECRET=local-dev-cookie-secret-change-me-0123456789

# --- Logging and proxy awareness --------------------------------------------
# Log verbosity: debug, info, warn or error. Logs are JSON on stdout, one
# object per line, so that Dozzle can render them as fields.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# How many reverse proxies sit between a client and this server. This decides
# whether X-Forwarded-For is believed, and both wrong answers fail silently:
#
#   0   nothing proxies you - the peer address is the client, and any
#       X-Forwarded-For on the request is ignored as the attacker-controlled
#       input it is. Correct for `make dev` and for `make compose-up`.
#   1   behind Nginx Proxy Manager, which is the deployed setup. Without this
#       every log line records the Docker bridge gateway instead of a client.
#
# Set it too low and a client can forge its own IP past the proxy's entry; set
# it too high and no client IP is recorded at all. See
# internal/web/middleware/clientip.go for why this is not a constant.
TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=0

# --- Postgres container ------------------------------------------------------
# Consumed by docker-compose.yml (ticket A4) and by the Makefile's derived
# DATABASE_URL. The container is only ever reachable on localhost.
POSTGRES_USER=nap
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=nap
POSTGRES_DB=nap
